---
scope: src/tools/info
status: aligned
description: >
  Lightweight boot-time tool that reports both AIDE artifact staleness (local versions vs. shipped manifest) and brain precondition state as a four-state tagged union (ok, no-brain-aide, no-mcp-entry, mcp-drift) so the orchestrator can branch on each.
intent: >
  aide_info is the passive boot-time precondition reporter the orchestrator
  calls once during its boot sequence. It surfaces two independent concerns
  in a single structured response. First, the existing staleness concern:
  it reads the host's .aide/versions.json from disk, compares each
  artifact's sourceCommit against the canonical manifest shipped inside
  the npm package, and returns which artifacts are outdated plus the
  server's npm version. Second, the brain-precondition concern: it
  forwards the result of `buildBrainState(root)` unchanged. The detector
  reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` via `parseBrainAide`, reads the host's
  `.mcp.json`, and compares the parsed `mcpServerConfig` against the
  `.mcp.json` brain entry. The tool returns one of four states — "ok"
  when brain.aide parses and `.mcp.json` agrees; "no-brain-aide" when
  brain.aide is missing or malformed; "no-mcp-entry" when brain.aide is
  valid but `.mcp.json` has no brain entry (or cannot be read); and
  "mcp-drift" when brain.aide and `.mcp.json` both exist but disagree on
  command/args. The package never validates the launcher path — that is
  the launcher's job at MCP server startup; the boot reporter does not
  stat any path on disk and surfaces no `invalid-path` state. The tool
  stays passive: it reports state, it never blocks, warns, or provisions
  anything. The orchestrator treats staleness as a soft notification and
  brain state as a hard gate — that policy lives in the orchestrator,
  not here. All failure modes are normal pre-init or misconfiguration
  states and must resolve to structured states, never thrown errors.
  The name stays aide_info (not aide_versions or aide_preconditions) to
  leave room for additional boot-time metadata in the future without
  renaming.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "Reads the host project's .aide/versions.json from disk and compares each artifact's sourceCommit against the shipped canonical manifest. Returns `{ serverVersion, outdated }` where outdated is an array of artifact keys whose sourceCommit differs (including artifacts present in canonical but missing locally)."
    - The response is small enough to add negligible cost to every orchestrator boot sequence. Only the list of outdated artifact keys, the server version, the brain-state object, and nothing else.
    - "The tool returns structured JSON, not a pre-formatted message. The orchestrator composes its own user-facing notification from the data."
    - "When the local .aide/versions.json is missing or unreadable, the tool returns `outdated: []` silently — it does not error or warn. Old installs that predate version tracking are handled gracefully."
    - "Forwards `buildBrainState(root)` output unchanged as the `brain` field of the response — no transformation, no field rewrite, no compatibility shim. The detector does the heavy lifting; this tool is a passive forwarder."
    - "Returns `status: 'ok'` when `.aide/config/brain.aide` parses successfully and the host's `.mcp.json` brain entry matches the parsed `mcpServerConfig`. The `ok` shape carries `name` (the user's human-readable label) and `hints`."
    - "Returns `status: 'no-brain-aide'` when `parseBrainAide` reports the file is missing, has malformed frontmatter, or has a malformed body. All three sub-cases collapse to this one status — without a usable brain.aide there is nothing to report and the user must fix or create the file."
    - "Returns `status: 'no-mcp-entry'` when brain.aide is valid but the host's `.mcp.json` cannot be read, cannot be parsed, or has no `mcpServers.brain` key. The shape carries `name` from brain.aide so the orchestrator can prompt the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`."
    - "Returns `status: 'mcp-drift'` when brain.aide is valid and `.mcp.json` has a brain entry, but the entry's `command` or `args` differ from the parsed `mcpServerConfig`. Drift is a hard halt — the orchestrator refuses to advance until the user runs sync."
    - "The brain status set is exactly four states — `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`. There is no `invalid-path` state because the package never stats the launcher path; path resolution is the launcher's job at MCP server startup, not the boot reporter's."
    - "`BrainState` carries no `backend` field and no `rootPath` field — the consumer surface is structured around the four statuses and the descriptive `name` label the user wrote in brain.aide. The path the launcher will use lives inline inside `mcpServerConfig.args` under user ownership; the boot reporter never extracts, derives, or surfaces it as a separate field."
    - "Brain state and staleness are returned as two independent top-level fields in the response. The orchestrator can read and branch on each without them interfering."
    - "The MCP tool description registered for `aide_info` at server-registration time is an auditable contract surface, not commentary. It accurately enumerates the four-state brain status union (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`), the two-field response shape (`outdated` array plus `brain: { status, name?, hints }`), the soft-notification-vs-hard-gate orchestrator policy split, and the no-disk-stat invariant — so an agent that reads only the description forms the same expectations it would form by reading this spec. The description, the handler's runtime return shape, and the rest of this spec stay in lockstep; drift between any two of them is a falsifiable QA failure traceable to this outcome."
  undesired:
    - A tool that requires the agent to read the local versions file and pass it in. The tool has the project root and reads the file itself.
    - A tool that triggers or recommends an upgrade. This is a passive notification surface; the orchestrator decides what to say.
    - A response that includes the full versions manifest. Only the outdated keys, server version, and brain-state object are needed.
    - A tool that throws or errors when the brain is simply not configured. Missing MCP config, missing `brain` entry, malformed brain.aide, and drift between brain.aide and `.mcp.json` are all normal pre-init or misconfiguration states and must resolve to structured brain-state values, not exceptions.
    - A tool that returns a pre-formatted "brain missing" message instead of structured state. Message composition belongs to the orchestrator, not this tool.
    - "A tool that attempts to provision the brain, scaffold a vault, or write to the host's MCP config file. This tool is strictly read-only — detection and reporting only, no mutation."
    - "A tool that surfaces brain state as part of the `outdated` array. Staleness is a soft notification and brain state is a hard precondition gate — they are distinct concerns and must be returned as distinct fields."
    - "A tool that conflates `no-brain-aide` with `no-mcp-entry`. `no-brain-aide` means the user's source-of-truth config file is absent or broken; `no-mcp-entry` means the config file exists and is valid but the derived .mcp.json wiring is missing. Different states, different remediations — collapsing them loses the signal the orchestrator needs."
    - "A tool that silently auto-syncs `.mcp.json` from brain.aide when drift is detected. Every mutation of the host's MCP wiring is a deliberate user action through `npx aidemd-mcp sync`; the boot reporter's only allowed response to drift is to name it and surface `mcp-drift`."
    - "A tool that re-surfaces a `backend` field on the brain state. The `backend` discriminant is removed entirely from `BrainState`; re-adding it — as `backend: null`, `backend: 'obsidian'`, or any other shape — re-introduces the retired per-connector dispatch the parent spec explicitly removed."
    - "A tool that re-surfaces a `rootPath` field on the brain state, or any synthesized field derived from `mcpServerConfig.args` that names the launcher's working directory. The path lives inline in `args` under user ownership; introducing a parallel field re-creates the deleted second source of truth and re-opens the validation surface (`invalid-path`) the four-state collapse retired."
    - "A tool that re-introduces the `invalid-path` brain status — under that name or any rename. The package does not stat the launcher path, does not validate it, and does not surface a state for its absence. Path errors surface at MCP server startup when the launcher fails; the boot reporter's contract ends at brain.aide-vs-`.mcp.json` agreement."
    - "A tool that auto-fixes drift instead of surfacing it. Repairing drift on boot silently destroys hand-edits the user made (e.g. a temporary debug vault) and shifts the mutation surface the user controls out from under them. Drift must halt; only `npx aidemd-mcp sync` resolves it."
    - "A registered MCP tool description for `aide_info` that teaches a status name, return field, response shape, or behavioral guarantee the handler does not implement or this spec does not define — for example: enumerating a fifth status, naming a retired status (`invalid-path`), referencing a removed field (`backend`, `rootPath`), describing path validation the package no longer performs, or omitting one of the four current statuses. The description is read by agents at tool-discovery time and shapes how they call the tool; teaching the wrong contract there silently misroutes every call regardless of how correct the handler is. Description-vs-handler drift, description-vs-spec drift, and silently-stale description text after a contract change are all the same falsifiable failure."
---

## Context

The `/aide` orchestrator boots on every invocation by firing a fixed set of
reads in parallel — the canonical methodology docs, the intent tree, and
this tool. Two preconditions must be checked before any pipeline work runs,
and they are structurally different. Staleness — whether the host's
installed AIDE artifacts (docs, commands, agents, skills) match the
canonical manifest shipped with this npm package — is a soft signal: if
something drifted, the user should know, but the pipeline can still run.
Brain availability — whether the host has a `brain.aide` config file
declaring a brain and a `.mcp.json` that agrees with it — is a hard gate:
the research and QA phases persist domain findings and retros into the
wired brain, and without a correctly-wired brain those phases silently
lose their outputs. The orchestrator must branch differently on each:
notify on staleness, halt on any non-ok brain state.

Both concerns are detectable from read-only disk inspection and both
need to be known at the same instant — the boot sequence. Giving them
separate tools would double the boot-time MCP call count and force each
detector to re-resolve the same host context independently. Collapsing
them into a single passive reporter lets the tool produce both findings
from one pass. The orchestrator still branches separately on each,
because the data is returned as two independent fields, not fused into
a single verdict.

Passive is the load-bearing word. A missing brain.aide, a malformed
brain.aide, a missing `.mcp.json` brain entry, and drift between
brain.aide and `.mcp.json` are all normal pre-init or misconfiguration
states — the tool gets called on cold projects before
`npx aidemd-mcp init` has ever run. A detector that throws on these
states would turn every first-ever orchestrator boot into an error. A
detector that auto-provisions them or silently repairs drift would violate
the contract that brain configuration is an inherent user decision and
.mcp.json mutation is always an explicit user-run command. The tool
reports; the orchestrator decides; `npx aidemd-mcp init` and
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` remediate.

The path a launcher will use to find the brain's data lives inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args` inside brain.aide — there is no separate
`rootPath` field, and the package does not derive one. Whether that
path resolves on disk is the launcher's problem at MCP server startup,
not the boot reporter's. Surfacing an `invalid-path` state here would
re-introduce the validation surface the schema collapse explicitly
retired and re-couple this tool to a per-launcher convention it has no
business knowing.

## Strategy

**Return `outdated` and `brain` as two independent top-level fields, not
a single fused verdict.** The orchestrator applies different policies to
each — staleness is a soft notification ("N artifacts outdated — run
`/aide:upgrade`"), brain state is a hard precondition gate ("brain not
configured — run `npx aidemd-mcp init` before continuing"). Fusing them into one
boolean or one status enum forces the orchestrator to unpack what the
tool already knew; keeping them independent means each branch in the
orchestrator reads only the field it cares about. This traces to the
desired outcome that brain state and staleness are returned as two
independent top-level fields so the orchestrator can branch on each
without interference.

**Staleness detection stays exactly as today: read
`.aide/versions.json`, compare each artifact's `sourceCommit` against
the canonical manifest, return `outdated[]` and `serverVersion`.** The
canonical manifest ships inside the npm package (read via
`readVersionsManifest` in `src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta/`) so the
comparison is always local — no network. When the host's
`.aide/versions.json` is missing or unreadable (an old install predating
version tracking, or a project that never ran init), the tool returns
`outdated: []` silently. A pre-init project is not "stale" — it simply
has nothing to compare — and surfacing a phantom warning would train
the user to ignore the notification.

**Brain detection is fully delegated to `buildBrainState(root)`.** This
tool calls the detector and forwards its output unchanged. The detector
reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` via `parseBrainAide`, reads `.mcp.json`,
and compares the parsed `mcpServerConfig` against the actual `.mcp.json`
brain entry. The info tool never reaches into brain.aide directly,
never dispatches through a backend registry, and never branches on
`name` — that coupling has been retired entirely. Forwarding unchanged
keeps the two surfaces in lockstep: when `buildBrainState`'s shape
evolves, the info tool's response shape carries it automatically without
a second edit here.

**Brain state is returned as a four-variant tagged union.** The four
states (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) each name a
distinct user-recovery path — create the file, run sync, run sync.
Collapsing any two states costs the orchestrator its ability to compose
the right remediation. The vocabulary deliberately omits `invalid-path`:
the package no longer carries a `rootPath` field, no longer derives a
launcher path from args, and no longer stats anything on disk to
validate it. Path resolution is the launcher's job at MCP server
startup, surfaced by the launcher's own error, not by this boot
reporter. This traces to the desired outcomes that enumerate exactly
four states and forbid re-introducing `invalid-path`.

**`no-brain-aide` is distinct from `no-mcp-entry`.** `no-brain-aide`
means the user's source-of-truth config file (`.aide/config/brain.aide`)
is absent or malformed — there is nothing to check downstream from it.
`no-mcp-entry` means brain.aide is valid, but the derived `.mcp.json`
wiring is absent or unreadable — the source of truth exists but hasn't
been synced yet. Conflating the two would route a user with a
present-but-unsynced brain.aide to `npx aidemd-mcp init` (re-interview)
instead of `npx aidemd-mcp sync` (re-derive). This traces to the
undesired outcome that forbids conflating the two.

**`mcp-drift` is a hard halt alongside `no-mcp-entry`.** When
brain.aide and `.mcp.json` both exist but disagree on the brain entry's
`command` or `args`, the detector returns `mcp-drift`. The orchestrator
treats this the same way it treats `no-mcp-entry` for pipeline gating —
it does not advance until the user runs `npx aidemd-mcp sync`. The tool
never auto-repairs drift; the user's visible command boundary is the
only thing that mutates `.mcp.json`. This traces to the undesired
outcome that forbids auto-fixing drift and the desired outcome that
names `mcp-drift` as an explicit status.

**`BrainState` carries no `backend` field and no `rootPath` field.**
The old contract carried `backend: <driver.id>` as a connector
discriminant for consumers and `rootPath` as a server-derived launcher
target. Both fields are removed entirely. Consumers branch on `status`
alone; the user's descriptive `name` label from brain.aide is threaded
through on states that carry it. The launcher path lives inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args` byte-for-byte under user ownership and is not
re-surfaced as a separate field on the response. Re-adding either
field — under any name — re-introduces a retired contract. This traces
to the desired outcome that explicitly names the absence of both
`backend` and `rootPath` as part of the contract.

**The tool writes nothing, provisions nothing, pre-formats nothing,
and stats nothing.** Even when it detects a fully broken brain state,
it returns structured data and exits. Message composition belongs to
the orchestrator (which tailors its wording to the user's context),
remediation belongs to `npx aidemd-mcp init` and `npx aidemd-mcp sync`,
and path validation belongs to the launcher at MCP server startup.
This traces directly to the undesired outcomes that forbid
provisioning, pre-formatted messages, mutation as side effects of the
detection flow, and re-introducing the `invalid-path` validation surface.

## Good examples

A fully set-up project, brain configured and `.mcp.json` agreeing, all
artifacts current:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

A project whose brain is healthy but whose commands drifted after a
recent server upgrade:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": ["commands/aide/aide", "commands/aide/plan"],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

A cold project where `npx aidemd-mcp init` has never run — no brain.aide,
no MCP config file, no versions file:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "no-brain-aide",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

A project where brain.aide exists but the user has not yet run
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` to write the .mcp.json entry:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "no-mcp-entry",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

A project where the user edited brain.aide to retarget their brain but
has not yet run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` — brain.aide and .mcp.json disagree:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "mcp-drift",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

The orchestrator halts the pipeline and tells the user to run
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` to realign .mcp.json with brain.aide. The user's
brain.aide already names the new launcher invocation inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; the boot reporter does not extract or echo
that path — the user reads it from their own file when remediating.

## Bad examples

A tool that throws when `.aide/config/brain.aide` or `.mcp.json` is missing:

```
Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '.aide/config/brain.aide'
    at aide_info ...
```

Every first-ever orchestrator boot on a cold project crashes here. The
detection flow must absorb missing files as legitimate pre-init states
and map them to `no-brain-aide` or `no-mcp-entry` as appropriate, never
bubble them as exceptions.

A tool that returns a pre-formatted message instead of structured
state:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": "Brain is missing — run npx aidemd-mcp init to set it up."
}
```

The orchestrator cannot compose a message tailored to the user's
context, cannot distinguish between no-entry and drift, and is
reduced to parroting a string the tool decided on. Message composition
is the orchestrator's job.

A tool that collapses all four states into a single boolean:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "configured": false
  }
}
```

The orchestrator has no way to recommend the right remediation. A user
whose brain.aide is missing, a user whose .mcp.json is unsynced, and a
user with drift are all told the same thing — with no name, and no
differentiation between the three distinct recovery actions they need
to take.

A tool that conflates brain state into the `outdated` array:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": ["commands/aide/aide", "brain"],
  "brain": null
}
```

Staleness and brain availability have different severities — one is a
soft notification, the other is a hard gate. Folding them together
forces the orchestrator to scan the `outdated` array for the magic
`"brain"` sentinel and forks the gate policy inside the staleness
branch. Two fields at the top level avoids this.

A tool that surfaces a `backend` field on the brain state:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "backend": "obsidian",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

The `backend` field is removed from `BrainState` entirely. Re-adding it
— even as a convenience alias for `name` — re-introduces the
per-connector dispatch the parent spec retired. Consumers branch on
`status`; the user's `name` label is the human-readable descriptor.
No programmatic use of brain identity belongs in this package.

A tool that surfaces a `rootPath` field synthesized from
`mcpServerConfig.args`:

```json
{
  "serverVersion": "0.4.3",
  "outdated": [],
  "brain": {
    "status": "ok",
    "name": "obsidian",
    "rootPath": "/home/user/vault",
    "hints": []
  }
}
```

The `rootPath` field is removed from the schema entirely. The
launcher's path lives inline in `mcpServerConfig.args` under user
ownership, in exactly one place. Re-deriving and re-surfacing it on
the response shape recreates the second source of truth the schema
collapse deleted, and immediately re-opens the validation surface
(`invalid-path`) the four-state collapse retired.

A tool that re-introduces `invalid-path` by statting the path it
extracted from args:

```
aide_info called → parsed brain.aide → extracted path from args[1] →
stat(path) failed → returned brain: { status: 'invalid-path', ... }
```

Every step of this flow is wrong under the new contract: the boot
reporter does not extract a path from args, does not stat any path,
and does not surface an `invalid-path` state. Path resolution is the
launcher's responsibility — the launcher fails at MCP server startup
when its path is wrong, with a launcher-specific error the user
recognizes. The boot reporter's contract ends at brain.aide-vs-
`.mcp.json` agreement.

A tool that auto-fixes drift on boot:

```
aide_info called → detected mcp-drift →
overwrote .mcp.json brain entry from brain.aide →
returned brain: { status: 'ok' }
```

The tool is read-only. Auto-syncing `.mcp.json` as a side effect of
a boot-time call means every orchestrator boot becomes a potential
mutation, and hand-edits the user made (e.g. a temporary debug brain)
are silently destroyed the next time the server boots. Drift is a hard
halt — the only thing the boot reporter may do with drift is surface
`mcp-drift` and stop. `npx aidemd-mcp sync` is the one and only surface
that mutates `.mcp.json`.

## References

- `src/tools/info/index.ts` -- current staleness-only implementation
  establishing the `.aide/versions.json` read, `sourceCommit`
  comparison against the canonical manifest, and silent-empty-array
  fallback when the file is missing — inherited unchanged by the new
  response shape.
- `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` -- sibling spec for the upstream
  detector this tool forwards. Defines the brain-state contract, the
  source-of-truth pivot to brain.aide, the removal of the `backend`
  field, the no-registry/no-connector-dispatch invariants, and the
  never-throws guarantee — all directly inherited by this tool's
  passive-forwarder posture.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- the brain.aide parser spec.
  Pins the tagged-result contract (`kind: ok | missing |
  malformed-frontmatter | malformed-body`) that `buildBrainState`
  branches on; the `interpolateArgs` helper it calls for drift
  comparison; the never-throws guarantee. Anchors why `no-brain-aide`
  collapses all three parse-failure variants.
- `src/service/install/wireMcp/index.ts` -- fixed-key existence check
  on `mcpServers.brain` establishes the entry-presence pattern;
  malformed-JSON branch demonstrates the pattern of mapping parse
  failures to a structured state rather than throwing.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` -- `buildBrainMcpStep`
  defines how the fixed `brain` key is written into `.mcp.json`; aligns
  the info-side detection with the install-side provisioning so the two
  sides agree on what "entry present" means.
- `src/service/install/.aide` -- establishes that brain configuration
  is an inherent user decision always resolved through the
  `npx aidemd-mcp init` interview; confirms the info tool must not
  silently default or fallback to a brain configuration, only report
  state.
- `src/cli/init/.aide` -- confirms that the CLI defers brain
  provisioning entirely to the `npx aidemd-mcp init` run, so a
  project bootstrapped via `npx` lands in `no-brain-aide` state and
  the info tool must resolve that cleanly without erroring.
- `.claude/commands/aide.md` -- MANDATORY BOOT SEQUENCE step 6 calls
  `aide_info` and currently only branches on `outdated`; documents
  the policy difference (staleness = soft notification, brain state
  will be a hard gate) that drives keeping the two fields
  independent.
- `.aide/intent.aide` -- the parent intent establishes that AIDE is
  an opinionated pipeline with a brain dependency and that the server
  is a renderer, not a decision-maker; the info tool's passive-
  reporter posture and the four-state contract inherit directly from
  this — including the explicit removal of `rootPath`, `invalid-path`,
  and the retirement of code-level path validation.
